    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you dive a bit deeper into the specific intermediates formed after the Amadori rearrangement? I'm specifically looking at how the degradation of those products leads to the actual formation of melanoidins and the various volatile aroma compounds. It's getting a bit dense so any clarification on the pathway would help.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Amadori products, which are stable intermediate compounds, are not the final product, but rather the starting point for the truly complex, chaotic browning phase. These intermediates undergo a cascade of reactions, which is where the real flavor and color development happens. One critical pathway involves the degradation of these sugars, leading to the formation of highly reactive dicarbonyl compounds.\n\nThese dicarbonyls then participate in various reactions, including Strecker degradation, where amino acids react with these carbonyls to produce aldehydes—these aldehydes are the volatile aroma compounds that give cooked food its signature smell. Simultaneously, these intermediates polymerize and condense with other molecules, forming the large, nitrogen-containing, brown polymers known as melanoidins. This polymerization step is what gives the food its characteristic dark color.",
      "source": "target",
