    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That breakdown of the Strecker degradation is helpful. I'm trying to wrap my head around the unpredictability of those volatile compounds though. Sometimes the flavor profile just feels... off or completely different than expected even when the temperature is controlled. From a chemical standpoint, what are the main factors that cause that kind of sudden shift in the aroma profile during the later stages of the reaction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The unpredictability in the final aroma profile, even with controlled temperatures, is often due to the sheer number of parallel reactions occurring simultaneously in the later stages. The system is not a simple, linear process; it's a vast chemical soup where multiple pathways compete.\n\nOne major factor is the concentration and availability of specific precursor molecules, like certain amino acids or sugars, which can be unevenly distributed in the food matrix. Furthermore, the rate of secondary reactions, such as fragmentation and cyclization of the initial products, is extremely sensitive to minor variations in moisture content or the presence of trace contaminants, which can act as unexpected catalysts or inhibitors. These subtle shifts can dramatically favor the formation of one specific volatile aldehyde over another, leading to a completely different flavor profile.",
      "source": "target",
